## Ligand Redox Non-innocence in the Stoichiometric Oxidation of $\mathsf{M n}_{2}(2,5$ -dioxidoterephthalate) (Mn-MOF-74)
†Department of Chemistry, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, 77 Massachusetts Avenue, Cambridge, Massachusetts 02139, United States ‡Physical Biosciences Division, Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, Berkeley, California 94720, United States
\*S Supporting Information
wing to their tunability and ordered structures, MOFs have been investigated as promising heterogeneous catalysts ever since their emergence as permanently porous materials.1−7 These materials typically mediate reactivity either through their pores, which can host catalytically active molecules or nanoparticles,8−10 or through their skeleton. Examples of the latter include the incorporation of known homogeneous catalysts within the bridging ligands11,12 and the use of the metal nodes, or secondary building units (SBUs), as active sites.13 When the SBUs are involved, they are most widely explored as Lewis-acid rcartea.lyHstos,w5,e14v−er1,7bwechailue ereSpBoUrts froecusniinqgueonmorledcoulx r eanctiitviietsy, 8a,r1e9 their redox reactivity should uncover new chemistry. So far, no report has demonstrated the ability of the constituent metal ions to stoichiometrically transform from one formal oxidation state to another, one of the fundamental requirements for establishing reactivity patterns at MOF SBUs. Despite notable examples of redox catalysis reportedly mediated by the SBUs,20 only substoichiometric redox changes have been documented thus far at self-assembled MOF nodes.19−27
To address the surprising paucity of examples of stoichiometric redox events at SBUs, we sought to answer the following critical questions: (1) Can all of the metal centers in any MOF support a change in oxidation state? (2) Can the porous lattice support the geometry changes that accompany the redox processes?
We chose as our study system the family of materials known as $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{DOBDC}$ (MOF-74), which incorporates a variety of divalent ion2s.28,29 MOF-74 contains a high density of open metal sites lining one-dimensional cylindrical pores of ${\sim}1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter (Figure 1), which should shuttle reagents either in the gas phase or in solution. Each metal ion sits in a pentacoordinating alloxygen pocket comprised of weak field carboxylates and phenoxides. Weak ligand fields are attractive for oxidative reactivity studies because they promote high spin electron configurations that afford more accessible redox couples, as is often seen in natural metalloenzymes such as methane monooxygenases and various hydroxylases.30,31 Lastly, the MOF-74 structure type exhibits good thermal stability, allowing solvent to be evacuated to furnish unsaturated metal sites with retention of crystallinity and porosity.
A previously unrecognized feature of the system was the possibility of ligand participation in redox events. We surmised that the $p$ -hydroquinone unit could be oxidized to the quinone, thereby acting redox non-innocently and enhancing the reactivity at the metal center, similar to what has been documented in molecular catalytic system s.32−35 Herein, we show that these factors enable $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ to withstand reactions with small molecule oxidants in a way that is unprecedented among MOFs. This reactivity showcases a rare example of a stoichiometric redox event and the first instance of ligand non-innocence mediated by SBUs in MOFs.36−39
Through a modified procedure (see Supporting Information),40−42 w e were able to obtain multigram quantities of highquality $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ in single batches to explore its gas/solid oxidation chemistry. Our first experiments revealed that kinetically inert oxidants such as $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ did not react with the activated framework at room temperature, while more aggressive oxidants such as $\mathrm{~O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ caused decomposition. The reaction with $\mathrm{~O}_{3},$ introduced as a ${\sim}4\%$ (v:v) mixture in $\mathrm{~O}_{2},$ caused spontaneous combustion of the MOF with the concomitant formation of a black solid, even when the reaction was performed at $-77^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . IR spectroscopic analysis of the black material did not reveal any resonances in the $600{-}4000~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ region, suggesting that the organic portion of the framework had been completely consumed. PXRD analysis confirmed that this product was $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (Figure S8), a material noted for catalyzing the combustion of organics,43 whose formation may therefore contribute to a self-catalyzed decomposition. Although IR spectroscopy of a $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ -treated $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ sample revealed features reminiscent of the starting material, it also evidenced a general increase in peak widths and a number of important differences (Figure S9). Extensive collapse of the structure was revealed by PXRD (Figure 2) and $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption (Figure S10), which indicated poor crystallinity and porosity $\left(\mathrm{SA}_{\mathrm{BET}}=64.2\left(3\right)\right.$ ${\bf m}^{2}/{\bf g})$ , respectively.
We hypothesized that the aggressiveness of the oxidation reaction would be tempered through the use of solid reagents that could be used in non- or minimally coordinating solvents. For this purpose, we chose iodobenzene dichloride, $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2},$ which serves as a convenient solid-state substitute for gaseous $\operatorname{Cl}_{2}$ .44 Despite using this milder reagent, treatment of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ with excess $\mathrm{\PhICl}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}{:}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}=2{:}1\right)$ ) also produced a material that had lost almost all crystallinity (Figure 2) and porosity $\left(\mathrm{SA}_{\mathrm{BET}}=85.9\left(3\right)\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ (Figure S10).
We achieved a more controlled process when the stoichiometry was limited to one $\mathrm{cl}$ per Mn atom (i.e., $\mathrm{PhICl_{2}{:}M n_{2}D O B D C=1{:}1}\mathrm{\Omega}$ , which gave a material with $\mathrm{\DeltaSA_{BET}}$ $=353.3(9)~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure S10). A remarkable further improvement was made when the reaction was performed at $-35^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ when a much more gradual orange-to-brown color change of the starting material could be visibly followed. Analysis of the oxidized brown product revealed a highly crystalline material with distinguishable diffraction peaks well past $2\theta=30^{\circ}$ (Figure
2). IR analysis of this product also revealed minimal changes in peak shape and position, with the exception of a notable new band at $\bar{1657}\mathrm{cm}^{\bar{-}1}.$ , a vibrational frequency indicative of a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ stretch (Figure 3).
Complete, stoichiometric oxidation of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ was confirmed by elemental analysis and a variety of spectroscopic techniques. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) confirmed the presence of chlorine and the absence of iodine (Figure S12), while combustion microanalysis revealed a precise 1:1 Cl:Mn stoichiometry, corresponding to a formula of $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{6})$ for $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}$ -treated $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ . To further confirm that $\mathrm{PhCl}_{2}$ was completely consumed in the stoichiometric oxidation of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}_{\cdot}$ the reaction was repeated in $\mathrm{CD}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2},$ and the formation of iodobenzene was followed by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR. This experiment indicated that $36\%$ of the $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}$ was consumed after $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ (Figure S2) and that the reaction was complete after $^{12\mathrm{~h},}$ when $97\%$ of the initial $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}$ could be accounted for as PhI (Figure S3). Since $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}$ is known to decompose at elevated temperatures, we monitored the rate of $\mathrm{{PhCl}}_{2}$ decomposition with and without $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ by $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR to confirm that we were observing MOF-assisted PhI formation. Based on the relative ratios from integrating NMR signals, we observed that $100\%$ of $\mathrm{{PhCl}}_{2}$ converts to PhI after $6\mathrm{~h~}$ in the presence of $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C},}\end{array}$ while only $28\%$ decomposes by itself (Figure S4).
To prove that all chloride atoms were bound to Mn ions, we compared the far-IR spectra obtained for both the pristine and the oxidized materials and observed a new resonance at 237 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , shown in the difference spectrum in Figure S13. This value is within the expected range of $_{\mathrm{{M-Cl}}}$ stretching frequencies.45 Additionally, a ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurement of $\mathrm{\dot{Cl}_{2}M n_{2}D O B D C}$ revealed a $\mathrm{\DeltaSA_{BET}}=58\mathrm{\hat{8}}(3)\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure S10), compared to $1353(4)~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for the unoxidized sample. When comparing two MOFs with cylindrical pores, the ratio of surface areas is equal to the ratio of pore diameter. Taking into account the van der Waals radii for Mn in the pristine sample and the sum of the ionic radii for Mn and Cl plus the vdW radii for chloride in the oxidized sample and assuming an interatomic column diameter of $15.4\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ the ratio of accessible surface areas would be 2.5, very consistent with the ratio of measured surface areas, 2.2. This implies a near perfect lining of the interior of the column with Cl bound to the pore-facing Mn ions.
Because all the data above pointed to a stoichiometric oxidation of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ and quantitative formation of $\mathrm{Cl_{2}M n_{2}D O B D C},$ , we expected that the oxidation took place at the $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ centers, with formation of $\mathrm{Mn}^{3+}$ . However, a magnetic susceptibility measurement of $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ revealed a room temperature magnetic moment, $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ of $5.55\mu_{\mathrm{B}},$ closer to the value of $5.92~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ expected for $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ with $S=5/2$ than to $4.90~\mu_{\mathrm{B}}$ expected for high-spin $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{3+}$ . More importantly, $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ for $\mathrm{Cl_{2}M n_{2}D O B D C}$ was nearly identical to the room temperature value obtained for the unoxidized, all- $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ sample $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ $\left(\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}=5.47\right)$ . We surmised that $\mu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ of both the oxidized and unoxidized samples were lower than expected for high-spin $\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ compounds due to antiferromagnetic coupling between neighboring $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ sites and neighboring $\mathrm{Mn^{2+}{-}O_{P h}{-}M n^{2+}}$ chains. Indeed, fitting the variable temperature susceptibility data for $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ to the modified Fischer model used previously for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}^{46}$ gave an intrachain coupling value, J, of $-2.3~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and an interchain value, $J^{\prime}.$ , of $-1.5~\mathrm{{\dot{c}m^{-1}}}$ (Figure S15 and Figure $^{4,}$ inset). These data suggested that despite overall two-electron oxidation of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}_{\cdot}$ , the formal oxidation state of Mn remains $+2,$ while $\mathrm{DOBDC^{4-}}$ is oxidized to its quinoid form, $\mathrm{DOBDC}^{2-}$ . Oxidation of the ligand substantiates the observed antiferromagnetic coupling since bridging $p$ -benzoquinone is known to mediate antiferromagnetic coupling in dinuclear complexes.47 The formation of a quinone also explains the new IR absorption at $1657~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , which we ascribe to a $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ bond, and which is indeed nearly identical to the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C}}={\mathrm{O}}$ stretch observed in neat $p$ -benzoquinone itself (Figure 3).
We turned to $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray techniques to unambiguously prove the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ oxidation state in $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ . Although XPS did not provide sufficient resolution for this purpose, Mn K-edge X-ray absorption spectroscopy revealed that the pre-edge features of both $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ and $\mathrm{Cl_{2}M n_{2}D O B D C}$ occurred at identical energies of $6539{\mathrm{~eV}}$ (Figure 4). Because the pre-edge feature is associated with a formally forbidden core-to-valence ( $(1s\rightarrow3d$ ) transition band, it should shift to higher energy upon oxidation of $3d$ metals. Here it does not, and the XAS data thus corroborate our hypothesis of ligand-based oxidation in $\mathrm{Cl_{2}M n_{2}D O B D C}$ .
To test the plausibility of the Mn ions mediating the ligand oxidation, we repeated the chlorination procedure with $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC},$ , a MOF-74 analogue with redox-inactive $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}$ . Although $\mathrm{PhICl}_{2}$ decomposition occurs in the presence of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ to a greater extent than in its absence, the rate of PhI formation was still slower than in the presence of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ . More importantly, treatment of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ with $\mathrm{Ph}\mathrm{ICl}_{2}$ did not result in the appearance of a carbonyl peak, as observed for $\begin{array}{r}{\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{D}\mathbf{C},}\end{array}$ and elemental analysis revealed that no $\mathrm{cl}$ atoms were incorporated into the MOF. Together, these demonstrate that $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ is not oxidized under these conditions and that the ligand remains in its reduced form.
We propose that the oxidation of $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}$ occurs by transient formation of a $\bf{M n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}\mathrm{-}\bf{C l}$ intermediate, which mediates the oxidation of the ligand and the formation of the $\mathrm{Cl-}$ $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (benzoquinone) thermodynamic product, presumably by sequential $1\mathrm{e}^{-}$ steps, as schematically proposed in Figure 5.
These observations describe the first example of a MOF where the ligands engage the metal ions through redox non-innocence in a manner typically associated with small molecule complexes.33,48,49 Because catechol/ $\overset{\cdot}{o}$ -benzoquinone ligands have been used to support redox catalysis,35 these results may also speak to the utility of deliberately incorporating redox noninnocent ligands into MOFs for more complex reactivity. Because the phenoxide groups in DOBDC4− also play an important structure-supporting role, their involvement in the redox process may be responsible for the observed material sensitivity and the irreversibility of the oxidation process.
Here we demonstrated that the metal nodes in microporous MOFs can be subject to clean stoichiometric oxidations. We also reported the first example of ligand redox non-innocence in MOFs, which may open a new area of exploration in these materials that parallels concepts of redox non-innocence in molecular complexes. During these studies, we established methods to interrogate the redox reactivity of MOF SBUs that maintain the integrity of the framework. These borrow from the synthetic toolkit of homogeneous (molecular) inorganic chemistry and may become general for future studies of SBU reactivity, a vastly underexplored subject in this otherwise popular area.
